  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather with gradually increasing \ntemperatures through the end of the week under high pressure.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure ridge axis remains over the area, and light winds\nare in store for the rest of the week. Temperatures will \ngradually increase, settling out in the 80s Saturday and Sunday.\nClear skies and calm winds tonight will allow for effective \nradiational cooling, and lows could dip into the lower 50s\nthroughout the forecast area. This may lead to patchy river \nvalley fog over the area tonight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Potential for a system moving in early next \nweek, bringing another round of wetting rainfall.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure lingers over the region this weekend, as low \npressure east of the Carolinas moves northeast into the Gulf of \nMaine by Monday. Meanwhile, another low pressure over Canada \nmoves north of the state with a cold front approaching the \nregion Monday afternoon. Some showers and storms are possible\nahead of it in the North Woods but generally thinking the chance\nis low. A greater chance of a wetting rainfall is Tuesday night\ninto Wednesday as another low pressure system approaches from \nthe west, but there is still uncertainty in system intensity \nand location. The chance of at least 0.5 inches of precipitation\nis currently only around 30 to 40 percent.",
